  "hints": [
    "Put one front door in front of the API: it should check who is calling and cap how fast each sender can go. Which component does all of that?",
    "The API should only accept and enqueue. Workers must remove at least 9,000 jobs/s, with headroom.",
    "Application Gateway (2) → API (10) → Kafka → Delivery Workers (6) → NoSQL (3), plus API → Redis → NoSQL for inbox and preference reads."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 40,
    "costBudget": 2200
  },
  "explanation": [
    "The Application Gateway terminates TLS, verifies service tokens and enforces a per-sender rate limit, so one runaway service can't spam users or overload the API. It also load-balances across API replicas.",
    "Accept fast, deliver later: the API validates and appends to a durable log, and returns. Workers consume at their own pace and retry failures with backoff.",
    "Consumer capacity must exceed the producer rate, or lag grows forever. Size workers for the peak with headroom.",
    "User preferences and recent inbox items are read constantly, so a cache keeps that traffic off the history database.",
    "Real systems add per-user frequency caps (don't send 50 pushes a minute), deduplication keys, and dead-letter queues for messages that keep failing."
  ]
}
